            .docGeneral: "hop – the little word for a quick, light move. one click – and everything you need is here. the set is yours: switch on the modules you want, leave the rest off, and spread them over up to four tabs:\n\n• timer – presets, work-rest cycles, time set by mouse or keyboard.\n\n• awake – the Mac keeps running and never dozes off, even with the lid closed\n\n• monitor – live system stats with custom thresholds.\n\n• clipboard – copy history with instant paste.\n\n• converter – compresses and converts images, PDFs, video, audio and documents in batches.\n\n• windows – snaps windows to halves, quarters and thirds of the screen.\n\n• time tracker – tasks, grouped into projects when you want them grouped, with the figure for today, this week or all of it; every stretch of time is listed and can be corrected.\n\n• to-dos – a simple checklist; completed items sink to the bottom.\n\n• vpn – every vpn this Mac knows about, each with its own switch; off stays off, and the vendor's window opens only when you ask for it.\n\n• apps – a grid of the programs you keep at hand, three to nine icons across, as many grids as you like.\n\n• removing apps – takes an app together with everything it left in a couple of dozen places, or cleans up caches, installers and leftovers without removing anything.\n\n• header – the icons up top switch between spaces (up to four, arranged in settings); {sym:gearshape} the gear opens settings, ⏻ quits the app.\n\n• internet – one-tap network speed via macOS's own networkQuality (Apple CDN servers).\n\n• off until you ask – torrents, the color eyedropper, text recognition, file archives and the keyboard lock. settings → modules & tabs is where any module is switched on and off again.\n\n• permissions – a tab of their own in this window: what each one is for, whether macOS has granted it, and what Hop never does. Nothing is asked at launch.\n\n• menu bar – the asterisk is hop itself; a blinking bell means the timer has finished, and the marks in its corners are explained just below.\n\n• handbook – this page and every module's own text live in settings → handbook, and each module repeats its part under its own settings.\n\n• your own ai agent can drive hop – claude code, codex or any script. ask it to add a task with a reminder, start a timer, track time, lock the keyboard or snap a window, and it writes a small json file that hop performs at once. hop also publishes what it is doing to a second file, so the agent can read the state before it decides anything. the files sit next to hop's data, and hop.dev's docs/automation.md describes them.",
            .docTimerFull: "timer and stopwatch: counts down any length you set, from the presets or typed in yourself, and runs work and break cycles like 25/5.\n\n• presets – 5 and 15 are breaks, 25 a pomodoro, 45 an academic hour, 60 an hour, 90 a deep-work cycle. your own presets live in settings.\n\n• dragging – pull the digits with the mouse: over hours it changes hours, over minutes minutes, over seconds seconds.\n\n• typing – click a digit group (hours, minutes or seconds), it lights up yellow, then type: digits slide in from the right.\n\n• restore – a preset tapped mid-countdown saves the running timer; the {sym:arrow.uturn.backward} button brings it back and resumes.\n\n• alert – sound, banner or silent: picked in timer settings.\n\n• stopwatch – the {sym:stopwatch} icon to the right of the presets switches to counting up from zero: start, pause and reset work the same, and the count also shows in the menu bar.\n\n• sharing the menu bar – when a task is being tracked at the same time, the two clocks take turns on the bar's one line: each holds it for five seconds and hands over with a fade, and a small glyph in front of the digits says whose time it is. a clock stopped from the panel keeps its place instead of leaving a gap.\n\n• {sym:playpause} start and pause buttons – the big circles: play starts or resumes (after the finale – restarts), pause holds the countdown, stop resets to the set time.\n\n• ±5 – the «min» capsules add or take five minutes, mid-run too.\n\n• done – the signal fires once; the digits keep blinking as a reminder it rang, right up until you reset. clicking the digits returns the timer to its set time.",
            .docAwakeFull: "holds sleep off for 15 minutes to ∞, keeping the screen lit if you ask, so a download, a build or a render finishes without a nudge of the mouse. the lid can be closed and the Mac carries on working.\n\nwhen it helps – a long download or build; agents and scripts that must not be interrupted; watching a movie without nudging the mouse; running with the lid closed next to an external display.\n\ndisplay stays on – also keeps the screen lit, not just the system.\n\nrun with lid closed – close the lid without shutting the Mac down: the screen goes dark, everything keeps running (asks for an admin password).\n\nthe yellow moon and the dot on the menu bar icon show it's active. turn it off and normal sleep returns.\n\n• {sym:lid} lid icon – the rightmost button in the row: click enables closed-lid mode (asks an admin password; turns sleep-block on if it was off), click again turns it off. amber = on.",
            .docClipboardFull: "everything you copy stays here: text, links, file paths and the files themselves. an entry goes back on the clipboard, straight into the app in front of you, or out as a txt, md, pdf or docx file on the desktop. passwords are not kept: a password manager marks what it copies as concealed, and concealed entries never reach the history.\n\n• bring back – click a row to put it on the clipboard again; the copy button copies, the insert button pastes straight into the active app.\n\n• files – a copied file is stored as its full path; clicking the row puts the file itself back on the clipboard: paste it in Finder as a file, or as the path in a text field.\n\n• {sym:square.and.arrow.down} save as a file – an entry can leave as a txt, md, pdf or docx file, named after its own text. switch it on in the clipboard settings, pick the format there, and say whether hop should ask for the name and the folder; without asking, files land on the desktop.\n\n• privacy – entries stay on this Mac only; concealed content (password managers) is never stored.\n\n• history size – how many recent entries to keep is set in the clipboard settings.\n\n• {sym:arrow.up.left.and.arrow.down.right} expand – the arrows next to the header stretch the list to 20 entries and shrink it back to 5.\n\n• clear – wipes the whole history at once.",
            .docConverterFull: "converts files between formats: heic, png, jpeg, avif, mp4, mov, mp3, wav, flac, docx, rtf, md, pages and pdf. changes resolution, compression level and aspect ratio, up to 500 files in one batch, with the size the result will come to shown before anything runs.\n\nimages – JPEG, PNG, HEIC, TIFF, GIF and RAW in; JPEG / PNG / HEIC out, plus AVIF for the web. format, scale and quality are adjustable.\n\nPDF – compression by redrawing pages (~150 dpi): files shrink severalfold, but the pages become pictures – text can no longer be selected or copied. controlled by quality.\n\nvideo – MP4, MOV, M4V in; MP4 or MOV out at original / 1080p / 720p / 540p. the container stays as it came, an mp4 leaves an mp4, and a small video is never blown up into a bigger one.\n\nframe – a clip can be reshaped for wherever it is going: 9:16 for reels and stories, 4:5 or 1:1 for a feed, 16:9 for everything else. footage of another shape is cropped to the middle, fitted with bars, or laid over a blurred copy of itself – crop, bars or blur, whichever you pick.\n\ncompression – a dial from 1 to 100 sets the bitrate hop encodes with, so squeezing a video really does make it smaller. the size promised before the run is arithmetic, bitrate times duration, and lands within a few percent of the file you get.\n\naudio – MP3, WAV, FLAC, AAC → M4A (AAC). the system has no MP3 encoder, so nothing comes out as MP3.\n\nfor – one button sets a clip up for where it is posted: the frame, 1080 across the short side, and the squeeze the platform itself recommends at 1080p30 (5 Mbps for reels and feed posts, 8 for youtube, shorts and tiktok). change any of those three and the button unlights – it stores nothing of its own.\n\nMKV and WebM – macOS opens neither, so hop repacks them into MP4 first: the picture is copied across untouched, which takes a second, and everything above then applies as to any mp4. a small helper downloads once for that. a webm whose video is vp8 cannot go into MP4 at all, and says so instead of quietly re-encoding.\n\niWork – pages, numbers and keynote documents are exported by pages, numbers and keynote themselves: to pdf, or to each one's own office format (docx, xlsx, pptx). the application has to be installed and macOS asks for permission at the first export; a file whose application is missing simply fails while the rest of the batch carries on. what comes out is exactly what «export to…» in that app produces – formulas included.\n\ndocuments – MD, DOCX, DOC, RTF and TXT in; PDF, MD or DOCX out.\n\nthe size estimate is honest: for images and PDFs the first file is trial-converted; for video and audio it's calculated from duration and bitrate.\n\neverything is local, files never leave the Mac. results go to Downloads, next to the original or a custom folder – originals stay untouched.\n\n• file rows – thumbnail, name and each file's own «now → after» size, and a video row says what it is becoming, «718p → 404p»; the circle turns into a green check when done. the bar moves with the bytes actually written rather than in jumps, a sound says the batch is finished, and «reveal in Finder» opens the folder with the file already selected.\n\n• ↗ – the converter row in the panel opens this window; the queue survives closing it.",
            .docConverterDocs: "documents – markdown, word files and pdfs turned into one another, in the same window and the same batch.\n\n• md → pdf – laid out by Hop itself: headings, lists, quotes, code blocks and links, on A4 pages with proper margins. the markdown engine is ours, so the result looks deliberate rather than like a browser printout.\n\n• word → pdf or md – .docx, .doc and .rtf: text, headings, emphasis, lists, simple tables and images come across. columns, footnotes and headers do not: those are layout, and the conversion is honest about losing them.\n\n• pdf → md / docx – switch the pdf group to md. a pdf stores layout rather than structure, so this is text EXTRACTION with heading guesses: a text-first document comes out well, a magazine spread comes out as prose. a scanned page with no text layer is read with Vision, the same engine the text recognition module uses.\n\n• md → docx – for when a document has to arrive in a shape Word will open. links become plain text there: the system's own word writer has nowhere to keep the address.\n\n• offline – everything is native: no converter service, no bundled office suite, nothing to download.",
            .docWindowsFull: "the active window snapped into halves, quarters, thirds, two-thirds, the centre or the whole screen, by a click in the panel or by a hotkey of its own, on whichever display it is on.\n\nthe buttons act on the window of the last active app. on first use macOS asks for the accessibility permission – without it other apps' windows can't be moved.\n\nlayout – in settings: the full 3×6 grid or a compact row of six essential zones.\n\nif a window doesn't fit a zone exactly, that app has a minimum window size macOS won't shrink past.\n\n• glyphs – each button is a mini screen: the filled part is where the window goes. the first row is the essentials – halves, center, full screen.",
            .docSpeedFull: "download, upload and responsiveness under load (RPM), measured by macOS's own networkQuality against Apple's servers, with the last result kept in the panel.\n\n• reading it – ↓ download and ↑ upload.\n\n• response – how many times a signal gets to a server and back in a minute while the line is fully loaded: below 100 calls and streams will suffer, 800+ is great. shown in the result's tooltip.\n\n• why live numbers move – the test ramps up parallel streams and loads the link both ways, so live figures float; the final number is the settled capacity. it's usually below speedtest.net peaks – those measure an idealized link to your ISP's own server.\n\n• staleness – a result older than 30 minutes (or taken on another Wi-Fi) turns dim: time to re-measure.\n\n• download in practice – 10 Mbps is enough for browsing and music, 25 for HD video, 50+ for 4K and quick app downloads, 100+ for heavy files without waiting.\n\n• upload in practice – 5 Mbps carries a call with screen sharing, 10+ HD video calls and cloud backups, 25+ comfortable streaming and big uploads.\n\n• low RPM on a fast line – RPM is delay under load, not speed. «under load» means the line is busy with something else: a file downloading, a cloud backup, someone at home streaming. in those moments router queues (bufferbloat) hold packets. 0–100 – on a busy line, voices in calls drop out and sound distorted, pages take seconds to open; 100–400 – only short hiccups under load; 400–800 – load is barely noticeable; 800+ – calls stay smooth even next to torrents. if you rarely load the line, low RPM won't bother you; the cure is a router with smart queue management (SQM).",
            .docTorrentFull: "a torrent client on an open-source engine: downloads torrents and magnet links, shows speed, peers and the time left, and takes only the files inside that you actually want. seeding stops by itself at ratio 1.\n\n• adding – drop a .torrent onto the card, paste a magnet link, or open a .torrent from disk. make Hop the default for .torrent files and magnet links and they open here straight from the browser or Finder.\n\n• choosing files – the add sheet lists what's inside; tick the files you want (select or deselect all at once) and confirm. a multi-file torrent expands into a per-file list you can toggle live, each with its own progress.\n\n• {sym:arrow.down.circle} downloading – the row shows percent, size, speed and eta; the download glyph lights up only while bytes are actually arriving. {sym:pause.fill} pause and resume any time.\n\n• {sym:checkmark.circle.fill} done and seeding – a finished torrent keeps seeding (sharing) to others; pause the row to stop, resume to share again.\n\n• stop at ratio 1.0 – off by default; turn it on in settings to stop seeding automatically once you've uploaded as much as you downloaded.\n\n• speed limits – cap download and upload rates (KB/s) in settings; 0 means unlimited. switch the fields between KB/s and MB/s.\n\n• disk space – before a download starts, the free space on the target volume is checked against the selected files; it won't begin if they don't fit.\n\n• where files land – the download folder (Downloads by default, changeable in settings). single-file torrents go straight in; multi-file ones get their own subfolder named after the torrent. {sym:folder} on a finished row reveals it in Finder.\n\n• {sym:xmark} removing – delete the torrent but keep the files on disk, or delete it together with the downloaded files.",
            .docColorFull: "pick any colour on the screen and copy it in one of three notations: hex, rgb or hsl.\n\n• {sym:eyedropper} picking – the loupe magnifies whatever is under the cursor, so a single pixel is easy to hit. escape cancels and copies nothing. no screen-recording permission is involved: the system loupe hands back one color and nothing else.\n\n• three notations at once – every color in the list shows hex, rgb and hsl side by side, and each one is a button: click hex to copy hex, click rgb to copy rgb. the list keeps the order the colors were picked in and never reshuffles under the cursor.\n\n• the list is settings – how many colors to keep and how many rows to show before scrolling live in settings → other modules, the same pair the clipboard has.\n\n• they are clipboard entries too – a picked color sits in the clipboard history as well, searchable there like anything else you copied.",
            .docOcrFull: "text and qr codes taken off the screen or out of any picture: a paused video, a protected pdf, a dialog that will not let you select, a frame from a game. line breaks survive, so a table or a code snippet stays readable, and the result is editable before it is copied.\n\n• {sym:text.viewfinder} two ways to start – \"select on screen\" hands you macOS's own crosshair to frame an area; the window also takes a picture dropped onto it or pasted with ⌘ V. printed and rendered text of any kind works: a paused video, a protected pdf, a dialog that will not let you select, a frame from a game. qr codes and barcodes are read in the same pass.\n\n• the result is visible – a window shows the text instead of quietly filing it away. edit it, select a line, or use the copy button; the text is already in the clipboard history either way, searchable there later.\n\n• line breaks are kept – a table or a code snippet stays readable instead of collapsing into one run-on line.\n\n• recognition – the Vision engine built into macOS does it, right on your own computer: nothing is uploaded anywhere and no internet is needed.\n\n• languages – nothing to choose: recognition works out the writing system itself, so a page in japanese, chinese, korean, russian, arabic or several of them at once is read as it is.\n\n• screen recording – macOS asks for it once, because framing an area means reading pixels; a dropped picture needs no permission at all. escape cancels a selection and copies nothing.",
            .docArchiveFull: "unpacks zip, rar, 7z, tar, tar.gz, tar.bz2, tar.xz and gz. packs files and folders into a zip, a tar.gz or a 7z, and the result lands on the desktop, beside the original, or in a folder of your choosing.\n\n• {sym:archivebox} the window – the module's row opens it, and that is where you drop things: a panel closes the moment a drag starts, so it could never hold a drop target. ⌘ V works too, several files at once – copy them in Finder and paste them here.\n\n• the list and the button – everything you add is listed, and nothing happens until you press unpack (or pack). archives in the list are unpacked; anything else is packed into ONE archive, so a mixed set packs.\n\n• where it lands – the Desktop by default, because a result has to appear where you are already looking. next to the original, or any folder you choose, is one click away in the same window.\n\n• formats – zip, rar, 7z, tar, tar.gz, tar.bz2, tar.xz and gz. rar and 7z fetch the 7-Zip helper (~6 MB) the first time one turns up, installed only after its signature checks out. Hop unpacks rar but never creates it: the format is proprietary.\n\n• packing format – zip, tar.gz or 7z, offered only when the list is going to be packed: an archive being opened comes out as whatever it already is. an existing name is never overwritten; the new one is numbered the way Finder does it, and several loose files come out inside one folder named after the archive.\n\n• opening from Finder – only rar can be assigned to Hop in settings → other modules, and never while an Apple app already owns it. Finder-opened archives unpack beside themselves, regardless of the window's destination setting. A successful double-click stays out of the way; the archive window opens only if the job fails. Earlier associations can be returned to Archive Utility from the same settings section.",
            .docKeylockFull: "every key goes dead for 1, 5 or 15 minutes, or until you say otherwise, so the keyboard can be wiped while the Mac keeps working: brightness, volume and media keys included, and the mouse or esc + shift brings it back.\n\n• {sym:keyboard} how it starts – the module has no start button: tapping a length starts it. 1, 5 or 15 minutes, or ∞ to stay locked until you say otherwise.\n\n• while it is on – a full-screen cover explains what is happening, and the menu-bar star turns into a keyboard so the reason the keys are dead is visible at a glance. every press, release and modifier is swallowed, including the brightness, volume and media row. nothing is recorded: the events are dropped, never read.\n\n• four ways out – none of them a shortcut: the done button on the cover, the unlock button in the panel, simply opening Hop's panel, or holding esc + shift for five seconds if the mouse is out of reach. unlocking plays a short cue.\n\n• the power button – a short press is swallowed like everything else, but holding it still forces the Mac off: that is handled in hardware and never reaches an app. Touch ID keeps working too.\n\n• accessibility permission – the same one the window manager uses, because swallowing keys system-wide is exactly what it governs. without it the keys would keep firing, so Hop asks before promising a lock.",
            .docVpnFull: "every vpn configuration macOS knows about, switched from the panel, with a light that says which tunnel is up and a warning when one is up but nothing is going through it. nothing to add: whatever registers itself in system settings shows up here, whatever the vendor.\n\n• switch – flips the connection straight from the panel. the light is green while the tunnel is up and dims while it is coming up or going down. it turns orange when the tunnel is up but nothing is coming back through it.\n\n• the name is a button – it brings up that vpn's own window when you need it (pick a country, change a setting), and hop quits the app again once you close the window. the connection stays: the tunnel is held by the system, not by the app. so the client never sits in the dock or the menu bar for a switch you touch twice a week.\n\n• the dot – with the panel closed the menu bar says the same thing: green while a tunnel is up, orange while one is carrying nothing. it can be switched off in settings; the list and its switches go on working.",
            .docUninstallFull: "an app removed together with the thirty-odd places it left files in: caches, preferences, containers, launch agents, logs, receipts. or a clean-up that removes no apps at all: caches by app, leftovers of apps long gone, old installers and the trash, biggest first. everything goes to the trash, so a mistake costs a restore.\n\n• {sym:trash} remove the app – drop an app on the row or pick it from the list of everything installed; each row shows what it weighs, the bundle and its data apart. around thirty places are searched: application support, caches, preferences and byhost preferences, containers and group containers, application scripts, saved state, http storages, webkit, logs, crash reports, cookies, autosaved data, launch agents and daemons, privileged helpers, plug-ins of every kind, receipts, the shared folder.\n\n• an app already in the trash is still recognised – its identifier is read from the bundle sitting there, or worked out from the leftovers that spell it out.\n\n• nothing is deleted – everything moves to the trash, so a mistake costs a restore rather than a file. whatever could not be touched is listed with the reason: containers, autosaved data and cookies need full disk access, and the window opens the right pane of system settings for you.\n\n• {sym:sparkles} clear the cache – the same window, removing nothing: every app holding a cache, biggest first; installers left in downloads, on the desktop and in documents; the data of apps removed long ago; and the trash with its size. one tick takes a whole section.\n\n• what it leaves alone on purpose – a container holding cache and data in one folder, a messenger's twenty gigabytes among them. only the app's own cleanup knows which half is disposable, so it is listed with its size and no tick.",
            .docMonitorRows: "everything the Mac is doing, in one place: processor, memory, disk, battery and temperature.\n\ncpu – processor load and the hottest spot of the chip. on Apple Silicon the die sensors are shared between CPU and GPU, so a separate GPU temperature is often unavailable.\n\ngpu – graphics load.\n\nmemory – used/total, counted like Activity Monitor.\n\nnetwork – current speed: ↓ download, ↑ upload.",
            .docMonitorRows2: "disk – used/total, percentage and SSD temperature.\n\nbattery – charge and temperature. health – capacity left versus factory (95% = the battery holds 95% of new); cycles – full recharges.\n\npower – adapter wattage plus current charge/draw. the charge figure below the adapter's rating is normal: part of the power runs the Mac itself, the battery gets the rest, and past ~80% the charge current tapers on its own to protect the battery.\n\nuptime – how long the Mac has run since the last reboot.",
            .docMonitorColors: "value colors: white or green – fine, yellow – heavy load, red – critical. thresholds for temperature, load, disk and battery are set in settings, along with °C or °F.\n\ngraphs – the switch in monitor settings turns rows into live charts: a filled area under each row shows how the value changed over the chosen time window.",
            .docTrackerFull: "time tracking by task: make tasks, group them into projects, and keep count of how long each one took — today, this week and all time. any stretch of time can be edited or deleted afterwards.\n\n• today / week / total – the switch above the list picks which stretch every figure covers, and a project carries the sum of its own tasks.\n\n• projects – «new project» sits at the right of the add row; drag a task onto a project to put it inside, even a folded one, and below it to take it out again. deleting a project deletes its tasks and their time, and asks first.\n\n• history – open a task and every stretch of time it collected is there: change how long one ran or when it happened, add a session nobody pressed play for, or throw one away. hand edits sit in the same list, so the lines always add up to the total above them.\n\n• start / pause / ✓ – the round button on the left starts a task; several tasks can run at once, each counting its own time. from then on the row counts the run you are in, from zero, and pause holds it without ending it. the ✓ beside the figure closes the run: what it collected stays in the history, the row goes back to the period's sum, and the next start counts from zero again. while a run is open the figure is the run, so editing the sum waits for the ✓.\n\n• in the menu bar – a running task shows its time up there too. when the timer is running as well, the two take turns on the bar's one line, five seconds each, with a small glyph in front of the digits saying whose time you are looking at.\n\n• rename – double-click a task's name to change it.\n\n• edit the time – click the total to type it (H:MM:SS, H:MM, or just minutes), or drag across it to scrub ±1 minute per step; the change is saved as a single correction.\n\n• forgot to stop? – once a task has run for over 8 hours, a gentle still-tracking row appears with its own stop button.\n\n• reorder – drag a row up or down to reorder the list.\n\n• {sym:xmark} delete – hover a row to reveal the ✕, click it, then confirm.\n\n• visible rows – in settings, cap the list to 3…15 rows (default 10); above the cap it scrolls, and the 8-hour warning stays pinned in view.\n\n• open a task – click a row to expand it: the full name, a description of your own, and a star for a favourite. settings can float starred tasks to the top.",
            .docTodosFull: "the list of what has to be done, with a description under each task and a reminder at the day and time you set, repeating on the weekdays you pick.\n\n• add – click the + new task row, type, and press return.\n\n• check off – click the circle to complete a task; completed tasks sink to the bottom, dimmed and struck through. unchecking a task returns it to its place among the active ones.\n\n• reorder – drag a row to reorder; active and completed tasks each reorder within their own group.\n\n• {sym:xmark} delete – hover a row to reveal the ✕, click it, then confirm.\n\n• visible rows – in settings, cap the list to 3…15 rows (default 10); above the cap it scrolls, with the add row always in view.\n\n• open a task – click a row to expand it: the full text on the first line, a description under it, the icons below. return adds a line, ✓ or ⌘ return saves, escape cancels.\n\n• remind – the bell sets a day and a time; the squares repeat it on the weekdays you pick. when it comes due you get a banner with snooze and done, a sound, and a mark in the menu bar – each one switchable in settings. a reminder that already went off leaves its time struck through in the list.\n\n• star – marks a favourite; settings can float starred tasks to the top.",
            .docAppsFull: "grids of the programs you open ten times a day, one click from the menu bar: nine icons across, arranged by dragging, and as many grids as you want to keep apart.\n\n• add – press + on the grid and pick an app, or drag one in from finder.\n\n• arrange – drag an icon and a yellow line shows which two icons it will land between; the rest shuffle around it, like a home screen. nine across, up to eight rows.\n\n• edit – the second button opens edit mode: the icons wobble, each gets a ✕ that removes it, and the grid can be given a name of its own. done leaves that mode.\n\n• names – the names under the icons can be switched off while editing, leaving bare icons.\n\n• as many grids as you like – add one in settings or under the module table, and delete one with the ✕ on its chip there. a new grid is empty and says so until you fill it.",
